        <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-kicker">COMMON QUESTIONS</span>
            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <p>
              Bottleneck results are estimates, so the useful question is not whether a PC has a perfectly
              fixed percentage, but which component is more likely to limit the workload you care about.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details>
              <summary>What is a CPU and GPU bottleneck?</summary>
              <p>
                A bottleneck is the part of a workload that limits performance first. In a game, the CPU may
                limit frame preparation or simulation work while the GPU may limit rendering. The limiting
                component can change with the game, resolution, graphics settings, and target frame rate.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Are bottleneck percentages exact measurements?</summary>
              <p>
                No. A bottleneck percentage is a comparative model estimate, not a universal hardware sensor
                reading. PC Balance therefore shows an estimate confidence and expected range rather than
                treating every result as equally certain.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Why can another bottleneck calculator show a different number?</summary>
              <p>
                Different tools use different benchmark suites, normalization methods, assumptions, and
                thresholds. Two calculators can disagree on the percentage while still identifying the same
                likely limiting component. PC Balance focuses on direction, magnitude, context, and confidence.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does resolution affect CPU and GPU bottlenecks?</summary>
              <p>
                Yes. Lower resolutions and very high frame-rate targets can expose CPU limits more often,
                while higher resolutions generally increase GPU rendering pressure. The exact change still
                depends on the game or application.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does target FPS matter?</summary>
              <p>
                Yes. A 60 FPS target and a 240 FPS target can place very different demands on the processor.
                If high-refresh gaming matters to you, selecting a target FPS gives the calculator more useful
                context.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does RAM affect the result?</summary>
              <p>
                RAM capacity can affect multitasking, content-creation workloads, and some games, but it does
                not replace CPU or GPU performance. PC Balance uses RAM as supporting context rather than
                pretending memory capacity alone determines a CPU–GPU bottleneck.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Is a GPU bottleneck always bad?</summary>
              <p>
                No. In many gaming workloads it is normal for the GPU to be the limiting component because the
                graphics card is doing most of the rendering work. What matters is whether performance meets
                your target and whether the mismatch is large enough to justify an upgrade.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>How should I use the upgrade recommendations?</summary>
              <p>
                Treat them as a shortlist, not a purchase guarantee. Before buying, confirm motherboard socket
                support, BIOS compatibility, power-supply capacity, physical dimensions, cooling, and local
                pricing. Retailer buttons are search links rather than live-price or stock guarantees.
              </p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Why do some older parts have lower estimate confidence?</summary>
              <p>
                Older hardware is not always present in the same modern benchmark suites as current parts.
                When data has to be bridged or calibrated from older references, PC Balance lowers the estimate
                confidence to reflect that additional uncertainty.
              </p>
            </details>
          </div>
